Hi Jairo

Thank you so much for getting back to me so quickly. I have a few other questions in response to your feedback.

I am analysing hip-worn accelerometry data, and correct me if I am wrong, that the current set-up (HASIB.algo = "Galland2012", Sadeh_axis = "Y") is only for classifying wake/sleep episodes; and I'd also need to use the "HorAngle" algorithm to define the sleep period time windows? So I should modify my script as below:

# Part 3 + 4
  #=====================
  HASIB.algo = "Galland2012,"
  Sadeh_axis = "Y",

  sensor.location = "hip",
  HASPT.algo = "HorAngle",

  longitudinal_axis = 2,

  excludefirstlast = FALSE,
  includenightcrit = 16,
  #=====================

If I remember correctly, the HorAngle algorithm hasn't been validated yet. Do you know if any studies have used this algorithm to detect the sleep period time window?

My other question is, if I'd like to use ENMO metrics for the classifications of activity intensities (but using Galland algorithm for sleep), should I also include acc.metric="ENMO" in the script?

And thanks for pointing out that I've put in the incorrect cutpoint for threshold.lig.

Thank you.

Best regards,
John.

On Wed, May 3, 2023 at 4:56 PM Jairo Hidalgo Migueles <jairo.hidal...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Hi John,
>
> The script looks good to me.
>
> I have never used the Galland algorithm before in my research, but this is how it is used in GGIR. If you are using hip-worn accelerometers, consider using the "HorAngle" algorithm to define de sleep period time windows (sensor.location = "hip", HASPT.algo = "HorAngle", longitudinal_axis = 2, the longitudinal axis should be the one aligned with the spine [vertical axis], which in most brands I have seen it is the second column in the raw data, but it would be good if you double-check this in your data).
>
> The cut-points look good, I see you are using the Evenson cut-points as provided in the manuscript, and you are using the same epoch length they used in the calibration study. Very minor point is that the calibration study reported a threshold.lig of 26, not 25, but I can't see this having a strong effect in your analyses.
>
> Hope this helps.
>
> Best,
> Jairo
